## Common SPL patterns for this app

### Pattern A — Site-grouped device health summary

```spl
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:devicehealth"
  earliest=-1h DeviceFamily!="Unified AP"
| eval Site=`dnac_site_normalize(coalesce(Site, Location))`
| where Site!=""
| stats
    avg(InterDeviceLinkAvailHealth) as avg_link_avail,
    avg(InterfaceLinkErrHealth) as avg_link_err,
    avg(OverallHealth) as avg_overall,
    sum(IssueCount) as open_issues,
    count(eval(Reachability!="Reachable")) as unreachable,
    dc(DeviceID) as devices
    by Site, DeviceRole
| sort Site DeviceRole
```

### Pattern B — Site-grouped issue pulse

```spl
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:issue" earliest=-24h
| eval SiteNameHierarchy=`dnac_site_normalize(SiteNameHierarchy)`
| where SiteNameHierarchy!=""
| eval issue_type=case(
    match(IssueName,"(?i)is down on network device"),"01_link_down",
    match(IssueName,"(?i)is unreachable"),"02_device_unreachable",
    match(IssueName,"(?i)high input/output utilization"),"03_high_utilization",
    match(IssueName,"(?i)high input/output (discard|error)"),"04_errors_discards",
    match(IssueName,"(?i)power supply"),"05_power",
    1==1,"99_other"
  )
| stats count by SiteNameHierarchy, issue_type
| sort SiteNameHierarchy issue_type
```

### Pattern C — Site-grouped client traffic & latency

```spl
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:client" earliest=-1h
| eval siteHierarchy=`dnac_site_normalize(siteHierarchy)`
| where siteHierarchy!=""
| stats
    sum(traffic.rxBytes) as total_rx_bytes,
    sum(traffic.txBytes) as total_tx_bytes,
    avg(traffic.rxRate) as avg_rx_rate,
    avg(traffic.txRate) as avg_tx_rate,
    avg(latency.voice) as voice_lat_ms,
    avg(latency.video) as video_lat_ms,
    avg(latency.bestEffort) as be_lat_ms,
    avg(traffic.txDropPercentage) as drop_pct,
    avg(traffic.rxLinkErrorPercentage) as rx_err_pct,
    dc(macAddress) as unique_clients
    by siteHierarchy
| sort -total_rx_bytes
```

## Anti-patterns (do not write code like this)

```spl
# WRONG — invented lowercase shadow field
| eval site_raw=coalesce(Site, Location)
| eval site=`dnac_site_normalize(site_raw)`
| stats count by site

# WRONG — using lowercase 'site' against 'Site' field
| stats count by site

# WRONG — using `index=...` directly instead of macro
search index=fdem_catalyst_index sourcetype="cisco:dnac:devicehealth"

# WRONG — using `search index=` with macro (macro already has index=)
search index=`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:devicehealth"

# WRONG — inventing fields that do not exist in the TA
| stats avg(InterfaceUtilizationPercent) by Site

# WRONG — wrong-sourcetype field name (Site does not exist on cisco:dnac:client)
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:client" | stats count by Site

# RIGHT — overwrite the canonical field, group by canonical name
| eval Site=`dnac_site_normalize(coalesce(Site, Location))`
| stats count by Site

# RIGHT — macro at start, no `search` prefix, no `index=`
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:devicehealth"
```

## `cisco:dnac:site:topology` — Site Hierarchy (NOT Network Topology)

**Onboarded on FDEM as of 2026-05-08.** Critical naming caveat: despite the
sourcetype's name, this is the **site hierarchy** API (areas / buildings / floors),
**NOT** the network device-and-link topology. There are no device records, no
neighbor (CDP/LLDP) records, no `linkStatus` fields. Treat this as a
**slow-changing reference dataset / lookup-like source**, not an event stream.

### Dual-schema warning (CRITICAL — discovered 2026-05-09)

This sourcetype is overloaded by the upstream Cisco TA: **two completely
different payload shapes share the same sourcetype name**. Always filter
explicitly so you target only the schema you mean.

| Schema | Distinguishing field | Example `name` values | What it represents | Where it shows up on this stack |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Hierarchy snapshot** | `id` (UUID), `nameHierarchy`, `type IN ("global","area","building","floor")` | `"TLH"`, `"First Floor"`, `"Florida"` | Periodic dump of the site tree (one event per hierarchy node) | FDEM's actual TA input. Polls re-emit identical events; expect 9× duplication per ~hour. |
| **Event-style change feed** | `eventId` (e.g. `"NETWORK-DEVICE-1-301"`), `type="NETWORK"`, `details.siteName`, `severity` 1/2/3 | `"device_removed_from_site"`, `"topology_link_added"`, `"site_topology_change"` | Audit/change events about the topology | Cisco's stock TA demo feed. Continuously streams synthetic events. |

**Filtering recipes:**
- **Hierarchy intent** (lookup generators, GPS extraction, drift panels):
  ```spl
  sourcetype="cisco:dnac:site:topology" type IN ("global","area","building","floor")
  ```
- **Event intent** (recent-change tables, churn distributions):
  ```spl
  sourcetype="cisco:dnac:site:topology" | where isnotnull(eventId)
  ```

**Why this matters:** without these filters, a "recent topology changes" panel
will display floor names and building names as if they were change events
(because the SPL emits `name`, `siteType`, etc., and both schemas populate those
fields with very different values). The lookup generator's `dedup id` happens
to mask the dual-schema problem because event-style records have no top-level
`id`, but downstream stanzas relying on `name` / `siteType` fields can be
silently corrupted on any stack that ingests both schemas.

The `Requires_DNAC_Topology_Events` tag marks every stanza that depends on the
event-style schema specifically. The hierarchy stanzas don't need a
counterpart tag because hierarchy is the FDEM default.

### Polling cadence

Snapshot-style. All events typically share a single `_time` — the timestamp of
the last DNAC poll. Don't expect to be able to `timechart` against this
sourcetype. Build lookups or use it inline as enrichment. **Catalyst Center
re-emits the entire tree on every poll**, so a 9-poll window produces 9×
duplication of every node. The lookup generator must `dedup id sortby -_time`
to collapse this. Field-value drift between polls is empirically zero for
FDEM's stack (verified 2026-05-09 across 9 polling cycles).

### Field inventory (18 fields)

`address, cisco_catalyst_host, country, floorNumber, height, id, latitude,
length, longitude, name, nameHierarchy, parentId, rfModel, signature_id, type,
unitsOfMeasure, vendor_product, width`

### Per-`type` field availability matrix

The `type` field stratifies which other fields are populated. Always filter by
`type` when extracting fields; `latitude` is null on `area`/`floor`/`global` rows.

| `type` | Populated fields beyond core (`id`, `parentId`, `name`, `nameHierarchy`) |
|---|---|
| `global` | (root only — has only `id`, `name`, `type`) |
| `area` | (just the core fields — useful for hierarchy traversal) |
| `building` | + `latitude`, `longitude`, `address`, `country` |
| `floor` | + `floorNumber`, `width`, `length`, `height`, `unitsOfMeasure`, `rfModel` |

### Caveats observed in FDEM data

- **Floor dimensions are placeholder defaults**, not real measurements. Identical
  values across multiple floors (e.g. `595.4196 × 389.6426` for both TLH First
  Floor and SEOC First Floor) indicate the floors were added to DNAC without a
  real floorplan upload. Don't trust `width` / `length` / `height` for
  visualizations.
- **Co-located buildings share GPS and address**. TLH and SEOC both report
  `30.38617, -84.23846` and `2489 Shumard Oak Blvd, Tallahassee` — they're on
  one campus. AUB and Warehouse both report `28.152445, -81.808044` and `660 C
  Fred Jones Blvd, Polk City`. This validates the existing `siteId=tlh` and
  `siteId=aub` rollups in `[overview_site_*_health]` stanzas.
- **Orlando / MCO is NOT present** in FDEM's site:topology snapshot. DNAC is
  not currently managing the Orlando site. Worth raising with FDEM
  operationally.
- **`signature_id` is in the field list but not populated** on the events seen
  so far. May be a TA-standard field that's empty for this sourcetype.

### Recommended use patterns

#### Pattern A — Site GPS lookup for the map

```spl
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:site:topology" type=building
| eval Site=`dnac_site_normalize(nameHierarchy)`
| stats latest(latitude) as lat, latest(longitude) as lon,
        latest(address) as address, latest(country) as country
        by Site, name, nameHierarchy
```

Output one row per building with GPS coordinates and full street address.

#### Pattern B — Site hierarchy traversal (id → parentId chain)

```spl
`index_catalyst` sourcetype="cisco:dnac:site:topology"
| eval Site=`dnac_site_normalize(nameHierarchy)`
| where type IN ("building","floor")
| stats values(name) as children, count by Site
```

Walks the hierarchy to enumerate buildings/floors per logical site.

#### Pattern C — Data quality signal (sites in topology vs sites in our config)

Lookup-style join: the absence of a site in this sourcetype means DNAC isn't
managing it. Useful as a configuration-drift / coverage check on the dashboard.

### What this sourcetype does NOT give us

Confirmed absent (do not reference these fields against `cisco:dnac:site:topology`):

- ❌ Device records (no `family`, `role`, `softwareVersion`, `MACAddress`,
  `IpAddress`, `DeviceName`)
- ❌ Link / edge records (no `source`, `target`, `linkStatus`, `linkType`,
  `linkSpeed`)
- ❌ CDP / LLDP neighbor data
- ❌ Interface info (no port names, no per-port state)
- ❌ Time-series fields (everything is snapshot; `_time` is the last poll
  time, not when a network event happened)

For real device-and-link topology data, a separate DNAC API endpoint would need
to be onboarded as a different sourcetype (e.g. `cisco:dnac:physical:topology`
or `cisco:dnac:device:topology`). FDEM does **not** have this today.

## Cross-sourcetype joins on DNAC site UUIDs

A discovery from 2026-05-08: **`cisco:dnac:site:topology.id` is the canonical
join key for the entire DNAC site graph**. Every other DNAC sourcetype that
references a site by UUID resolves directly against `site:topology.id`. This
unlocks human-readable site labels on three sourcetypes that previously had
only opaque UUIDs:

| Sourcetype | Join field | Field shape | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cisco:dnac:client` | `siteHierarchyId` | `/<global_id>/<area_id>/<building_id>/<floor_id>` (slash-delimited UUID chain, leading and trailing slashes) | Extract leaf via `mvindex(split(rtrim(siteHierarchyId,"/"),"/"),-1)` |
| `cisco:dnac:client` | `siteId` | leaf UUID directly | Already the leaf — no extraction needed. **Note:** `siteId` and the leaf of `siteHierarchyId` can differ by a few events because DNAC sometimes places the same client at building precision (`siteId=building.id`) and sometimes at floor precision (`siteHierarchyId` ending in a floor UUID). Either field joins correctly to `site_topology_lookup`. |
| `cisco:dnac:clienthealth` | `siteId` | leaf UUID — **but see FDEM caveat below** | The ONLY site-reference field on this sourcetype. **FDEM caveat (2026-05-08):** every `clienthealth` event on FDEM has `siteId="global"` (literally the string), with NO per-site rollups emitted. Until FDEM/DNAC begins emitting per-site clienthealth, joining this sourcetype to the topology lookup will produce zero rows on FDEM. Demo data has the expected per-site UUIDs. |
| `cisco:dnac:audit:logs` | `network.siteId` | leaf UUID directly | Dotted JSON path, joins like a regular field |

### Canonical leaf-extraction recipe

For `cisco:dnac:client.siteHierarchyId` (the only chain-shaped field), always
extract the leaf with this exact pattern. `rtrim` is required because the
field has a trailing slash; without it, `split` will produce an empty string
as the last element.

```spl
| eval leafSiteId=mvindex(split(rtrim(siteHierarchyId,"/"),"/"),-1)
```

For `siteId` and `network.siteId`, no extraction is needed — pass the field
directly to `lookup`.

### Canonical join recipe via `site_topology_lookup`

The `site_topology_lookup` CSV (populated hourly by the `Executive Dashboard
- Site Topology Lookup Generator` saved search) is the standard join target.
It carries: `id, name, type, parentId, nameHierarchy, Site, lat, lon,
address, country, floorNumber, rfModel, cisco_catalyst_host,
inheritedFromBuilding, updated`. Floor rows are **enriched** at lookup-build
time so they inherit `lat`, `lon`, `address`, and `country` from their parent
building — meaning **every leaf UUID in the lookup has a complete address**,
regardless of whether the leaf is at building or floor level.

```spl
| lookup site_topology_lookup id as leafSiteId OUTPUT name as siteNodeName, type as siteNodeType, Site, address, lat, lon
```

Always call `lookup` AFTER aggregating by the leaf UUID, not before — this
keeps the lookup fan-out small and predictable.

### Aggregation pattern (collapse hierarchy levels into one Site)

DNAC places clients at varying granularity (some at building level, some at
floor level) depending on AP coverage. To produce one row per logical Site
regardless of which hierarchy level the leaf lives at, group by `Site` and
use `values(address)` to capture the address (rather than grouping by
`address` directly, which would split building-leaf and floor-leaf rows of
the same site into separate groups).

```spl
| stats sum(uniqueClients) as uniqueClients, sum(events) as events,
        values(siteNodeName) as siteNodes, values(address) as address
        by Site
```

### Anti-patterns

- ❌ **Don't** call `lookup` before the per-UUID aggregation. It produces
  redundant per-event lookup work and bloats the working set.
- ❌ **Don't** group by `Site, address` after the lookup. Floor-level rows
  would have a different `address` shape than building-level rows of the
  same site, splitting them into separate groups. Group by `Site` only and
  pull `address` via `values()`.
- ❌ **Don't** re-query `cisco:dnac:site:topology` directly inside dashboard
  searches. Use the `site_topology_lookup` CSV; it's faster and survives
  ingest gaps.
- ❌ **Don't** strip the leading slash with `ltrim` before splitting. Splunk's
  `split()` correctly produces an empty leading element, which `mvindex(-1)`
  ignores. The trailing slash is the only one that matters; use `rtrim`
  there.

